    {
      "type": "prose",
      "caption": "Vowels, diphthongs and sigma",
      "html": "<p>The vowels are α, ε, η, ι, ο, υ, ω: ε and ο are always short, η and ω always long, α, ι and υ either. Two vowels pronounced together form a diphthong: αι, ει, οι, υι, αυ, ευ, ου, ηυ. Sigma is written ς at the end of a word and σ everywhere else: σοφός, λόγος. On the course keyboard you always type σ; it becomes ς by itself when the word ends.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "caption": "Common confusion",
      "html": "<p>ν (nu) looks like a Latin v, η (eta) like an n, ρ (rho) like a p and χ (chi) like an x. Read them by their Greek names until the shapes stop fooling you. γγ is ng, as in ἄγγελος (angel).</p>"
    },
    {
